      "heading": "Reading the result honestly",
      "narration": "You have fitted a model. Now read it honestly, which is a separate skill and a rarer one. Start with R-squared, the fraction of the variance in y that your model explains. Zero means you did no better than always predicting the mean; one means perfect prediction. It is a useful summary, and it has a trap that catches people constantly: R-squared always goes up when you add a feature. Always. Even if the feature is a column of random numbers, it will find some accidental scrap of correlation to exploit, and your R-squared will improve. So never use raw R-squared to compare models with different numbers of features. Use adjusted R-squared, which charges you for each parameter, or better, forget both and compare the models on validation data as we did last lesson. Next, and this is the habit that separates practitioners from people who just call fit: plot the residuals. Take the errors and plot them against the predictions. If the model is right, you get a shapeless cloud centred on zero with no pattern in it. If you see a U-shape, the true relationship was curved and your straight line is systematically wrong at both ends and in the middle — add a squared term. If you see a widening funnel, the variance of the error grows with the size of the prediction, which means your model is most confident exactly where it is least reliable. These pictures tell you things that no single summary number can. Third, multicollinearity. When two of your features move together closely, the model can trade weight between them freely without changing predictions much, so the individual coefficients become unstable — refit on a slightly different sample and they can swing wildly or even flip sign. The predictions survive this perfectly well; the explanations do not. If you only need forecasts, ignore it. If somebody is going to act on the coefficients, deal with it. And finally the warning that matters most outside the classroom. Correlation is not causation, and a regression coefficient is a correlation. The phrase 'holding the others fixed' is only true for the features you actually put in the model. Any cause you left out is still there in the world, hiding inside the coefficient of everything it touches.",
